   288	### 3.4 Output
   289	
   290	Every report carries these six fields, and they are the stable set that later verifiers must reproduce: the verdict,
   291	its reason code, T, `revocation`, `historical_security` and standing. [A9:156; A9:262-268]
   292	
   293	- `revocation: NOT_EVALUATED`. [A9:248-252]
   294	- `historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED`. The documentation names what a later examiner must supply: an authenticated
   295	  safe-before cutoff per algorithm and per key, compared with T and A2.1's chain-time uncertainty. [A9:253-259]
   296	- Standing: `ABSENT/NOT_ISSUED_IN_PROFILE`, reported separately and never folded in. [A9:166-167; SPEC R-1.16:163]
   297	- No aggregate checkmark. [SPEC R-5.47:1419]
   298	- **Report encoding.** The stable-output object is the JCS object `{"historical_security":"NOT_EVALUATED",
   299	  "reason","revocation":"NOT_EVALUATED","standing":"ABSENT/NOT_ISSUED_IN_PROFILE","T","verdict"}`; the success
   300	  reason is `OK`. T is the RFC 3339 string of nTime(h*) when the receipt proof parses, its file digest equals SHA256
   301	  of the presented `a_sig` bytes, no attestation in it is contradicted, and it has a qualifying attestation;
   302	  otherwise `null`. T is reported whatever the verdict. Diagnostics (trust-config version and hash, spec hash,
   303	  per-check list) go in a separate wrapper object, outside the stable six. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G32]
   304	- **T for dispatch-terminal outcomes.** A D1–D4 outcome (section 3.1) evaluates nothing, the receipt proof
   305	  included, so its T is `null`; this holds for D4 too, even when the same bundle bytes under a strict request would
