docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:7:## Reviewed artifacts and validation
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:25:## Findings and exact fixes
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:27:### F01 — MEDIUM: the empirical conclusions drop the censoring and clock qualifications
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:37:> In every retained complete six-block window, at least one recorded arrival lag was at most 1064.9 s. The minimum computed U minus the inclusion block's recorded first-seen time was 6135.1 s; U's UTC interpretation remains conditional on A-UTC. If the selected observer readings do not understate production time, these observations support A-UTC for the sampled windows. Observer clock error is not bounded here. The dataset's ±7200 s quality filter and excluded incomplete windows prevent an inference about the frequency of A-UTC failures outside the retained sample.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:43:Replace the first conclusion bullet at evidence :149–152 with:
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:55:### F02 — MEDIUM: the MTP count does not establish what consensus forces when the six miners change their timestamps
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:71:> At the observed header history, 559173 of the 559718 complete windows contain a block with first-seen minus MTP at most 7200 s. Holding that block's actual predecessors fixed, and assuming its recorded arrival does not precede production, the MTP rule forces that block's timestamp within the tolerance. This is a diagnostic of the observed history. It does not show that the six miners could not violate A-UTC by changing their timestamps: earlier changes within the window can lower later MTPs. No count of windows requiring control outside the six is established here.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:75:> Consensus requires each timestamp to exceed the median of the preceding eleven header times. The evidence reproduces 545 windows in which every recorded first-seen-minus-MTP value exceeds 7200 s. These MTPs use the actual predecessor timestamps and the observer-clock qualification. The count does not bound a coalition changing the six timestamps, since changes within the window also change later MTPs. A majority coalition can sustain lagging timestamps; A-UTC is not consensus protection against that adversary.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:79:### F03 — MEDIUM: lowest-height selection does not eliminate favourable proof presentation
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:104:### F04 — MEDIUM: the plain custody rule excludes people rather than access paths
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:118:The existing following bullets give the procedure's contents. Explain AWS here at first use, as STE-STYLE rule 6 requires. The amendment's technical custody rule can stay as written.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:120:### F05 — LOW: row 24 conflates failure of a sufficient assumption with failure of the numerical bound
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:138:### F06 — MEDIUM: row 32 adds prerequisites, and the definition of GATE contradicts row 33
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:150:### F07 — LOW: the concatenated-data digest is not reproduced by the stated command
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:170:### F08 — LOW: the promised adjacent A-UTC qualification is not consistently present in standalone summary statements
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:192:## Checks without a further finding
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:194:**UTC construction and contract.** The proof is `e < r(h*) ≤ r(j) ≤ nTime(j)+7200 ≤ max(nTime(h*…h*+5))+7200` for the j supplied by A-UTC. Inclusion is confirmation one, so the segment ends at h*+5. No new bound on service observation delay B−C is inferred. Guarantee 2 remains a chain-time statement. The wrapper contains the assumption identifier, is null exactly when T is null, stays outside the six stable fields, and does not establish service authenticity when reported with INVALID or UNVERIFIABLE. No seventh stable field or changed A9.4 contract is introduced.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:198:**Key substitution.** The exact tuple match precedes overlap/horizon classification, including future prelisted matches. A shared endpoint is overlap. An unlisted tuple within an overlapping interval or the closed horizon is INVALID; a disjoint unlisted future interval is UNVERIFIABLE. Overall failure precedence remains in force. For append-only releases, the existing matching tuple, overlapping interval, or old closed horizon preserves every prior pass/INVALID conclusion of this check. A finite scratch enumeration of 117600 permitted single-addition comparisons found no change to such prior outcomes; this illustrates the construction, not implementation conformance or formal discharge. Pin extensions can still change selection and other results across different configurations.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:208:**Future scope and plain style.** The old automatic V2 obligation is withdrawn. TSAs, a witnessed log and unresolved examiner requirements remain recorded candidates/questions. Conditional prerequisites for an optional future rotation do not require that rotation to happen. The plain summary otherwise uses short sentences, parallel bullets, technical identifiers and visibly proposed glossary additions. Its material fidelity problems are F01/F04/F08; AWS also needs the first-use explanation supplied in F04. No new architectural mechanism or future project is required to resolve these findings.
