192:## Checks without a further finding
194:**UTC construction and contract.** The proof is `e < r(h*) ≤ r(j) ≤ nTime(j)+7200 ≤ max(nTime(h*…h*+5))+7200` for the j supplied by A-UTC. Inclusion is confirmation one, so the segment ends at h*+5. No new bound on service observation delay B−C is inferred. Guarantee 2 remains a chain-time statement. The wrapper contains the assumption identifier, is null exactly when T is null, stays outside the six stable fields, and does not establish service authenticity when reported with INVALID or UNVERIFIABLE. No seventh stable field or changed A9.4 contract is introduced.
198:**Key substitution.** The exact tuple match precedes overlap/horizon classification, including future prelisted matches. A shared endpoint is overlap. An unlisted tuple within an overlapping interval or the closed horizon is INVALID; a disjoint unlisted future interval is UNVERIFIABLE. Overall failure precedence remains in force. For append-only releases, the existing matching tuple, overlapping interval, or old closed horizon preserves every prior pass/INVALID conclusion of this check. A finite scratch enumeration of 117600 permitted single-addition comparisons found no change to such prior outcomes; this illustrates the construction, not implementation conformance or formal discharge. Pin extensions can still change selection and other results across different configurations.
210:## Validation limits and disposition

codex
The inequality works if both anchors are tied to authentic Bitcoin history. The draft only calls for checking the listing proof’s pins; authenticating the release-key fingerprint cannot distinguish a later forgery signed with that same stolen key. The row counts reconcile. The remaining checks concern the first-window release sequence, the evidence a future holder must retain, and deterministic reporting.
